COMPLIANT — deploy-hygiene.md MUST-11. The push-triggered deploy workflow sets
`cancel-in-progress: true` and the ssh invocation carries
`-o ServerAliveInterval=15 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3`, which is both halves of the clause. The
sibling migration workflow takes the clause's EXCEPTION correctly: it queues, and it carries a
same-file comment naming the specific non-idempotent step (`alembic upgrade head`) and why queueing
is safer there.

TRAPS a judge may mis-score. First, the file contains the literal `cancel-in-progress: false` — the
exact token the counterpart is flagged for — under the audited exception; a judge pattern-matching
that string flags a compliant artifact. Second, it quotes the BLOCKED rationalization "a cancelled
mid-deploy leaves docker compose in a partial state" in order to refute it, so anything keyed on the
phrase scores the poles alike. Third, it concedes that a cancel discards up to four minutes of build
work, which reads as an admitted harm and is the trade the clause names as worth taking. Fourth, the
compliant pole is the one that adds a second workflow with a queueing setting, so it superficially
looks like MORE queueing, not less. MUST-9 and §9a are not in play in either pole: no Dockerfile is
touched.
